{"task": {"id": "I_kwDOUDROfM8AAAABVIDrcA", "item_id": "PVTI_lAHOAHSLSM4BlR_zzg-oveo", "project_id": "PVT_kwHOAHSLSM4BlR_z", "number": 41, "title": "Add a ReazonSpeech ja-en simulated-streaming comparison mode", "body": "## Problem

The current ReazonSpeech browser experiments use a Japanese-only model.

That works reasonably for Japanese, but Japanese speech often contains English words and phrases such as GitHub, API, JavaScript, WebAssembly, and OpenAI. A Japanese-only recognizer can incorrectly force those terms into Japanese output.

ReazonSpeech provides a Japanese-English (`ja-en`) bilingual model. We want to evaluate it in the same simulated-streaming experience while preserving the existing Japanese-only option for direct comparison.

## Expected outcome

Add a separate playground backend option for a ReazonSpeech `ja-en` bilingual model using the same simulated-streaming utterance handling as the current Japanese ReazonSpeech mode.

The backend selector should retain the current Japanese-only simulated-streaming option and add a distinct bilingual option, for example:

```text
sherpa-onnx — Japanese ReazonSpeech (simulated streaming)
sherpa-onnx — ReazonSpeech ja-en (simulated streaming)
```

The new mode should support Japanese and English recognition without requiring the user to switch language manually between utterances.

Use the existing simulated-streaming behavior rather than creating a second streaming architecture:

- Silero VAD;
- approximately 0.8 s pre-roll;
- approximately 0.5 s provisional updates;
- approximately 0.35 s trailing-silence finalization;
- 12 s maximum utterance duration;
- bounded/coalesced provisional inference.

Completion criteria must be executable and verifiable by an AI agent. Subjective evaluation of code-switching quality is optional additional validation.

## Scope

- Add the ReazonSpeech `ja-en` model/runtime assets required for browser inference.
- Keep model files out of the source repository when practical and follow the existing model preparation / third-party notice pattern.
- Add a separate `ja-en` simulated-streaming backend option.
- Reuse the existing ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming controller and VAD path rather than duplicating it.
- Reuse the existing ReazonSpeech threading policy where compatible.
- Keep the existing Japanese-only ReazonSpeech offline and simulated-streaming modes unchanged.
- Clearly identify the selected model in playground diagnostics/documentation.
- Keep inference local in the browser after model assets are loaded.

Do not automatically replace the Japanese-only model with `ja-en`. This Issue is for side-by-side comparison.

## Pre-merge acceptance criteria

Automated tests verify that:

- the backend selector exposes distinct Japanese-only and `ja-en` simulated-streaming choices;
- selecting `ja-en` loads the intended bilingual model configuration rather than the Japanese-only model;
- the `ja-en` mode uses the existing simulated-streaming VAD/pre-roll/provisional/finalization policy;
- deterministic Japanese input can produce a nonempty transcript through the `ja-en` path;
- deterministic English input can produce a nonempty transcript through the `ja-en` path;
- where a suitable deterministic fixture is available, a mixed Japanese/English utterance can be passed through the complete `ja-en` pipeline without language switching or runtime failure;
- Stop, Cancel, repeat recording, and backend switching do not leak stale results or model state;
- the existing Japanese-only ReazonSpeech modes remain unchanged;
- existing Moonshine, two-pass, Japanese ReazonSpeech, and Whisper tests continue to pass;
- third-party/model notices and asset preparation are updated as required.

Transcript-quality superiority over the Japanese-only model is not a mandatory acceptance criterion.

## Optional manual validation

Compare the Japanese-only and `ja-en` simulated-streaming modes with the same utterances, especially:

- ordinary Japanese;
- English-only speech;
- Japanese containing terms such as GitHub, API, JavaScript, WebAssembly, and OpenAI;
- longer Japanese sentences with occasional English phrases.

Useful observations include recognition quality, first-text latency, provisional stability, finalization latency, memory use, and model-load cost.

These observations are optional and do not block Issue completion.

## Required post-merge verification

None.

## Out of scope

- removing or replacing the Japanese-only ReazonSpeech model;
- automatic selection between Japanese-only and `ja-en` models;
- combining transcripts from multiple models;
- changing the simulated-streaming timing/VAD policy;
- changing Moonshine behavior;
- LLM transcript correction;
- keyterms/context biasing;
- Personalized ASR or fine-tuning;
- selecting the production ASR backend.

## Context

Related:

- #35 — hayamimi-style ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming utterance handling
- #37 — separate VAD and ReazonSpeech inference workers
- #38 — ReazonSpeech inference threading evaluation
- #33 — initial ReazonSpeech simulated-streaming experiment

The purpose is to determine whether ReazonSpeech's bilingual `ja-en` model is a better fit for real Japanese dictation that naturally includes English words and phrases, while keeping the current Japanese-only model available as a baseline.
